TSM client.dll patch -- main menu ambient music (+ startup diagnostic)
========================================================================

What this is: a binary patch to Half-Life's TS client DLL (cl_dlls\client.dll) that plays a
looping mp3 track while the main menu is up, before the player joins a server, plus a temporary
diagnostic that records whether and when the hook function actually runs. This file documents
exactly what bytes changed and why, byte-for-byte, generated directly from a diff of the original
and patched files rather than written by hand -- so it cannot drift from what the patch actually
does.

Original file : client.dll
Patched file  : client_patched_v5.dll
Original MD5  : cfc60e4e96c51903e6ef7d2b9839b3b7
Patched MD5   : 0a787eae3642fd42a070c0be030fd40f
Original size : 861184 bytes
Patched size  : 865280 bytes (+4096)

Nothing in the original file's bytes is deleted or moved. The patch is: one 5-byte instruction
changed in place, a handful of PE header fields updated to describe a new section, and one new
4096-byte section appended to the end of the file. Every existing function, byte offset, and
relocation entry outside the one 5-byte hook is untouched.

This version (v5) differs from the prior v4 patch only by adding an unconditional diagnostic
write, described in section 3b. The menu-music logic itself (sections 3a, 4) is unchanged from
v4's design.


1. The hook -- HUD_VidInit, RVA 0x501f (file offset 0x441f)
-------------------------------------------------------------

HUD_VidInit is a function the engine calls once video mode is set up, early in startup, before
the main menu is shown. Its original code (unchanged apart from the one line below):

    0x5010  mov ecx, 0x100c9d28
    0x5015  call 0x10009d10
    0x501a  call 0x1003db50
    0x501f  mov eax, 1          <-- replaced
    0x5024  ret

The instruction at 0x501f is a 5-byte "mov eax, 1" (bytes: B8 01 00 00 00), which just sets
HUD_VidInit's return value. The patch replaces exactly those 5 bytes with a 5-byte call into new
code appended at the end of the file (bytes: E8 98 30 2D 00 -> call 0x102d8098... the exact target
below). Nothing else in HUD_VidInit moves: the "ret" at 0x5024 is still the original, untouched
byte. The new code ends with its own "mov eax, 1; ret", so it has the same observable effect on
the caller as the instruction it replaced, plus the side effects described below, and control
returns to exactly 0x5024 afterward, same as before the patch.

    file offset 0x441f-0x4423:
      original: b8 01 00 00 00    (mov eax, 1)
      patched:  e8 98 30 2d 00    (call into the new .mus section)


2. PE header changes -- registering the new section
-------------------------------------------------------------

  * File header, NumberOfSections: 7 -> 8
  * Optional header, SizeOfImage: 0x2d8000 -> 0x2d9000
  * New section table entry appended (into the slack that already existed after the 7 existing
    entries -- no existing entry moved):

      Name             .mus
      VirtualAddress   0x2d8000
      VirtualSize      0x1000
      PointerToRawData 0xd2400  (immediately after the original file's last byte)
      SizeOfRawData    0x1000
      Characteristics  0x60000020  (CODE | EXECUTE | READ -- not writable)

These are the standard, minimal edits to add a new section to a PE image; nothing about any
existing section (.text, .rdata, .data, .reloc, etc.) is touched.


3. The new .mus section (file offset 0xd2400, 4096 bytes, all new)
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Laid out as:

  0x2d8000  UTF-16 path, "ts\cl_dlls\vidinit_debug.txt" (diagnostic log, see 3b)
  0x2d803a  UTF-16 path, "ts\cl_dlls\menu_music.txt" (the track-list config, see 4)
  0x2d806e  ASCII fallback track name, "vangelis.mp3"
  0x2d807b  ASCII command template, "MP3Volume 1\nmp3 on\nmp3 loop sound/music/"
  0x2d80a3  ASCII diagnostic message, "HUD_VidInit fired, call #"
  0x2d80bc  the actual code (214 bytes); the rest of the section is unused zero padding

3a. The menu-music logic, run once (guarded by a flag so it cannot re-trigger on a later video
    mode change):

  1. Opens "ts\cl_dlls\menu_music.txt" (relative to the game's working directory, i.e. right next
     to this DLL) via CreateFileW/ReadFile/CloseHandle -- all three already imported by the
     original client.dll from KERNEL32.dll, so no new imports were added for this part either.
  2. If the file is missing, reads its contents (up to 4095 bytes) into a scratch buffer.
  3. Splits the contents into lines. A line is ignored if it is empty or its first character is
     '#'. Every other line is recorded as a candidate track filename.
  4. If zero candidates were found, the fallback name "vangelis.mp3" is used. If exactly one, that
     one is used. If more than one, one is chosen uniformly at random via the engine's own
     pfnRandomLong function.
  5. Builds "MP3Volume 1\nmp3 on\nmp3 loop sound/music/<chosen name>\n" and sends it exactly once
     via the engine's pfnClientCmd -- the same mechanism the TS server plugin (ts_matchmode.sma)
     already uses for its own in-map ambient jukebox.

3b. The diagnostic, run on EVERY call, with no guard (temporary -- for finding out whether and
    when HUD_VidInit actually runs, before the menu-music feature's own timing can be trusted):

  1. Increments a call counter kept in client.dll's own scratch memory (capped at 99 for display).
  2. Renders "HUD_VidInit fired, call #<NN>\n" into a small buffer.
  3. Overwrites "ts\cl_dlls\vidinit_debug.txt" with that line via CreateFileW (CREATE_ALWAYS) /
     WriteFile / CloseHandle -- the same three KERNEL32 imports as 3a, plus WriteFile, already
     imported by the original DLL as well.

  This file's presence (or absence) after a normal launch answers directly whether this hook
  point fires before the main menu is shown, and the call number shows roughly how many times.
  It is meant to be removed again once that question is answered.

All scratch buffers the code writes to at runtime (read buffers, the candidate list, the final
command string, the one-shot guard flag, the diagnostic counter) live in client.dll's own .data
section, in its existing zero-filled slack space -- no new writable memory was added; .mus itself
is marked read+execute only, consistent with it containing only code and constants.

Every address the new code touches -- the engine function table, the KERNEL32 imports, its own
constants, its own scratch variables -- is computed at runtime relative to the module's own
actual load address (via a standard position-independent "call/pop" sequence at the top of the
stub), rather than being a fixed address baked into the file. This means the new code needs no
new entries in the .reloc directory and works correctly regardless of where Windows actually
loads the DLL -- confirmed by checking that no relocation entry exists inside either the hook
bytes or the new section.


4. The config file
-------------------------------------------------------------

Location: ts\cl_dlls\menu_music.txt, next to client.dll itself.

Format: one mp3 filename per line (matching a file already in sound\music\). Blank lines and
lines starting with '#' are ignored. If the file is missing, or has no usable lines, the DLL
falls back to "vangelis.mp3". If it lists more than one track, one is picked at random every time
the game starts.

Changing the menu music later never requires touching the DLL: edit this text file.


5. Rollback
-------------------------------------------------------------

The original, unmodified client.dll (MD5 cfc60e4e96c51903e6ef7d2b9839b3b7) is kept alongside this
patch. Restoring it is a single file copy back over ts\cl_dlls\client.dll, with the game closed.
